## Topics

`attacks /` card testing, credential stuffing, scalping, discount abuse, gift card
brute forcing, scraping, fake accounts, click fraud, layer 7 flooding.
`defence /` WAF and bot management, rate limiting, velocity rules, device
fingerprinting, checkout and login protection, CAPTCHA conversion cost, app and plugin
permission risk.
`agents /` wanted versus unwanted agents, allowlists, agent directives, verified agent
traffic, agentic checkout.
`detection /` traffic anomalies, bot traffic in analytics, conversion rate anomalies
as a signal, chargeback spikes.
`response /` incident playbooks, disclosure obligations, cleaning polluted data.

## Skip

Enterprise frameworks with no ecommerce specifics. Vendor fear marketing with no
detection method. Blanket bot blocking that ignores wanted agents. Several near-
identical "threats and best practices" overviews: keep 1.

## Why it earns its place

Bot traffic corrupts the inputs the whole OS runs on. SEC findings feed 06-MEA as a
signal-quality input: a brand under an undetected attack should not get a profit
ranking at all.

## Scope mix

Real baseline: bot protection at checkout and login, rate limiting present, bot
traffic excluded from analytics, admin 2FA on, no app holding unaccounted write scope.

